1
00:00:00,340 --> 00:00:06,440
Aligning Incentives Pharmaceutical companies make more money treating you forever than curing you once.

2
00:00:07,220 --> 00:00:11,880
Defense contractors earn one thousand four hundred and twenty-five dollars per lobbying dollar.

3
00:00:12,720 --> 00:00:20,460
Nobody is evil. The incentives are. Rearrange the profit motive so curing disease pays better than causing it, and watch

4
00:00:20,460 --> 00:00:27,980
capitalism accidentally save your species. This manual is not about convincing people to be better.

5
00:00:28,380 --> 00:00:33,250
You've been trying that for six thousand years and it hasn't worked once.

6
00:00:33,860 --> 00:00:41,880
Every temple, sermon, philosophy lecture, and after-school special in your history has been an attempt to make humans behave

7
00:00:41,880 --> 00:00:48,890
nicely, and the result is your current situation, which I think we can all agree is not nice.

8
00:00:49,820 --> 00:00:58,380
This chapter is about something different. It's about building a system where doing well and doing good are the same thing.

9
00:00:58,920 --> 00:01:07,580
Every major interest group gets aligned behind curing disease, not through charity or guilt, but through pure profit.

10
00:01:08,680 --> 00:01:13,260
On Wishonia, we discovered that you can't change what organisms want.

11
00:01:13,860 --> 00:01:21,760
You can only change what's profitable. Then they change themselves, enthusiastically, and take credit for it afterward.

12
00:01:22,940 --> 00:01:27,320
Defense Contractors: Teaching Merchants of Death to Love Life.

13
00:01:28,520 --> 00:01:33,520
Military lobbyists spend one hundred and twenty-seven million dollars on lobbying.

14
00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:38,560
They get one hundred and eighty-one billion dollars in government contracts back.

15
00:01:39,320 --> 00:01:45,410
That's roughly one thousand, four hundred and twenty-five dollars returned per dollar invested.

16
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:51,870
Better returns than any hedge fund in history, and all you have to do is help kill strangers.

17
00:01:52,680 --> 00:02:00,660
They will oppose any treaty that threatens this. Not because they're evil (though that helps), but because murder is profitable

18
00:02:00,660 --> 00:02:06,580
and they like money. This is not a moral observation. This is an investment thesis.

19
00:02:08,460 --> 00:02:15,540
Lobbyists are like pigeons. They go where the food is. Currently the food is in opposing the treaty.

20
00:02:15,740 --> 00:02:22,250
You could try convincing them that saving lives is noble. Or you could just move the food.

21
00:02:22,540 --> 00:02:31,380
This is not a metaphor about pigeons. This is the entire strategy of this chapter, applied to every actor, in every section,

22
00:02:31,700 --> 00:02:39,140
until you get tired of hearing it. It's like convincing a vampire to open a blood bank; counterintuitive but mathematically

23
00:02:39,140 --> 00:02:49,900
sound. The Offer. You give them VICTORY Incentive Alignment Bonds paying two hundred and seventy-two percent annually, which

24
00:02:49,900 --> 00:02:57,240
is more than their current salary and stock options. You give them early access, which means they get rich before everyone

25
00:02:57,240 --> 00:03:03,030
else, which is the thing your species cares about even more than getting rich (getting rich first).

26
00:03:03,780 --> 00:03:11,560
You give them consulting fees, which is legal bribery with a name, and strategic advisor positions, which are fake jobs

27
00:03:11,560 --> 00:03:16,270
with real money. On Wishonia, we would find this arrangement absurd.

28
00:03:16,920 --> 00:03:21,260
On Earth, it's called "industry relations" and there are conferences about it.

29
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:30,700
The Math. Their current job (lobbying against the treaty): pays five hundred thousand dollars to two million dollars per

30
00:03:30,700 --> 00:03:37,320
year, depends on military budgets staying huge, and leaves a legacy of "made orphans for money."

31
00:03:38,020 --> 00:03:43,040
Your offer (lobbying for the treaty): five million dollars to twenty million dollars in bonds, returning two hundred and

32
00:03:43,040 --> 00:03:46,480
seventy-two percent annually for as long as the treaty holds.

33
00:03:47,440 --> 00:03:53,520
And since bondholders are billionaires with pre-existing influence over government, good luck repealing it.

34
00:03:54,240 --> 00:04:00,140
Legacy: "Accidentally saved humanity while getting rich." Most people can do this math.

35
00:04:00,720 --> 00:04:08,360
You hope. Why They Take It. Two hundred and seventy-two percent is more than whatever they're making now.

36
00:04:08,760 --> 00:04:15,060
That's it. That's the whole reason. On Wishonia, we spent years looking for a deeper explanation for human behavior.

37
00:04:15,440 --> 00:04:21,140
There isn't one. It's money. It's always money. The deeper explanation IS money.

38
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:29,340
The treaty doesn't actually hurt them. Military budget drops one percent (from two point seven two trillion dollars to two

39
00:04:29,340 --> 00:04:33,760
point six nine trillion dollars). They still have ninety-nine percent to protect.

40
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:41,940
Defense contracts still flow. Jobs stay. You're asking them to accept slightly less murder funding in exchange for becoming

41
00:04:41,940 --> 00:04:47,760
extremely wealthy. This is not a hard sell. This is barely a sell at all.

42
00:04:47,800 --> 00:04:56,940
It's more of a notification. Add ten percent VICTORY Incentive Alignment Bonds to a pension portfolio and blended returns

43
00:04:56,940 --> 00:05:00,930
jump from ten percent to something that looks like a typo on a spreadsheet.

44
00:05:01,460 --> 00:05:10,820
(Be suspicious. Then check the math.) Once Lockheed buys bonds, Raytheon has to follow or explain to shareholders why Lockheed's

45
00:05:10,820 --> 00:05:17,360
portfolio is outperforming theirs. Start with pension funds (fiduciary duty requires best returns).

46
00:05:18,040 --> 00:05:21,680
They end up lobbying FOR the treaty to protect their investment.

47
00:05:22,600 --> 00:05:31,000
Your species calls this "unintended consequences." On Wishonia, we call it "the intended consequence," because we intended

48
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:39,640
it, because that was the plan. What You're NOT Asking. You're not asking them to close factories, lay off workers, stop

49
00:05:39,640 --> 00:05:45,520
making weapons, convert to medical equipment, change their business model, or feel bad about anything.

50
00:05:45,600 --> 00:05:52,700
You're only asking them to accept higher returns on investment, stop blocking the treaty, and maybe say nice things about

51
00:05:52,700 --> 00:05:58,360
it publicly. They keep ninety-nine percent of their current income PLUS get rich from bonds.

52
00:05:58,880 --> 00:06:04,560
This is the kind of negotiation where both sides win and one side doesn't even realize it's a negotiation.

53
00:06:05,020 --> 00:06:09,240
On your planet, this is called "a good deal." On mine, it's called "obvious.".

54
00:06:11,140 --> 00:06:18,340
Why This Works. It's all legal. VICTORY Incentive Alignment Bonds are legal investment vehicles.

55
00:06:18,460 --> 00:06:22,940
Lobbying is protected free speech. Consulting fees are standard practice.

56
00:06:23,380 --> 00:06:30,100
They designed the system to allow this. You're just using it correctly for the first time, which is to say, you're using

57
00:06:30,100 --> 00:06:37,220
it for something other than weapons, which has never occurred to anyone before, which tells you everything about your species'

58
00:06:37,660 --> 00:06:42,620
relationship with its own legal system. The defense contractor C.E.O.

59
00:06:42,680 --> 00:06:49,300
explains it to the board like this: "We diversified into bonds paying two hundred and seventy-two percent returns versus

60
00:06:49,300 --> 00:06:53,760
the market's ten percent. If the treaty passes, we profit enormously.

61
00:06:54,600 --> 00:06:58,510
If it fails, we still have our defense contracts. It's a hedge.

62
00:06:58,920 --> 00:07:04,920
I'm being prudent. You're welcome." The board nods. The board always nods when the numbers are good.

63
00:07:05,280 --> 00:07:08,340
The board has never once asked whether the numbers were moral.

64
00:07:08,700 --> 00:07:13,140
This is, again, the design parameter. Here's what the C.F.O.

65
00:07:13,140 --> 00:07:20,900
tells the C.E.O. after the board meeting: the treaty is even better than a hedge, because fewer bases means less blowback

66
00:07:20,900 --> 00:07:29,420
means fewer terrorism crises. Robert Pape at the University of Chicago found that ninety-five percent of suicide terrorist

67
00:07:29,420 --> 00:07:37,300
attacks from nineteen eighty to two thousand and three were responses to foreign military occupation, and the eight trillion

68
00:07:37,300 --> 00:07:44,560
dollar War on Terror took attacks from about one thousand per year to nearly seventeen thousand by twenty fourteen.

69
00:07:45,440 --> 00:07:52,100
Crisis-driven budgets sound great for defense revenue, but they're terrible for long-term capital planning.

70
00:07:52,980 --> 00:07:57,790
Emergency appropriations spike procurement, then get clawed back when the panic fades.

71
00:07:58,200 --> 00:08:03,920
You can't plan a fifteen-year fighter jet program on a budget that lurches from crisis to crisis.

72
00:08:04,620 --> 00:08:10,440
The treaty replaces boom-bust panic cycles with stable, predictable bond returns.

73
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:17,980
A C.F.O. who can forecast revenue twenty years out sleeps better than one who depends on the next attack making the news.

74
00:08:18,680 --> 00:08:23,780
Fewer crises, more stability, higher Sharpe ratio. The C.F.O.

75
00:08:24,020 --> 00:08:31,160
doesn't phrase it as "world peace." The C.F.O. phrases it as "reduced volatility in our revenue model."

76
00:08:31,800 --> 00:08:39,640
Same thing. Different PowerPoint. Budget: Strategic allocation from the one billion dollar incentive alignment budget.

77
00:08:40,620 --> 00:08:49,540
The Timeline. Optimistic (if this works): key lobbyists flip in months one to twelve, defense contractor trade associations

78
00:08:49,540 --> 00:08:57,360
go neutral by month eighteen, and by month twenty-five former opponents take credit for supporting medical research, which

79
00:08:57,360 --> 00:09:01,940
they discovered they cared about yesterday but will swear they've cared about their whole lives.

80
00:09:02,960 --> 00:09:10,080
Your species does this with every social change. Opposition, resistance, capitulation, retroactive credit.

81
00:09:10,640 --> 00:09:17,320
It's the four seasons of human progress. On Wishonia, we have a word for the moment when someone who fought against a good

82
00:09:17,320 --> 00:09:22,960
idea starts claiming they invented it. The word is "creditworm."

83
00:09:23,040 --> 00:09:26,940
Your species doesn't have a word for it, which is why it happens so often.

84
00:09:27,760 --> 00:09:36,240
Things without names are invisible. Pessimistic (if this doesn't work): you and everyone you've ever loved slowly deteriorate

85
00:09:36,240 --> 00:09:40,700
into dust. You can't fight the military-industrial complex.

86
00:09:40,700 --> 00:09:45,680
It's too big. Too entrenched. Too profitable. But you might be able to buy it.

87
00:09:46,500 --> 00:09:53,380
Insurance Companies: The Accidentally Aligned Industry. Insurance companies are theoretically on your side.

88
00:09:53,780 --> 00:10:00,240
They've had this exact incentive for one hundred and fifty years and haven't done anything about it, which is the most human

89
00:10:00,240 --> 00:10:05,860
sentence I've ever written. Every disease you cure is billions they don't have to pay out.

90
00:10:06,540 --> 00:10:14,740
They are, on paper, your most natural ally. In practice, they've spent one hundred and fifty years not noticing this, which

91
00:10:14,740 --> 00:10:20,800
makes them your most natural ally in the same way that a person sleeping through an earthquake is technically experiencing

92
00:10:20,800 --> 00:10:26,800
an earthquake. Their Current Death Spiral. Medical costs rise seven percent annually.

93
00:10:27,420 --> 00:10:32,000
Premiums can't keep pace because of regulation. Claims are destroying margins.

94
00:10:32,440 --> 00:10:39,060
Chronic disease means lifetime payouts, which means every patient who doesn't die is a patient who keeps costing money,

95
00:10:39,480 --> 00:10:45,560
which means insurance companies are in the bizarre position of profiting when you die quickly and losing money when you

96
00:10:45,560 --> 00:10:51,360
die slowly, which is not what they put on their brochures but is what their actuarial tables reveal.

97
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:57,580
Their ideal customer is someone who pays premiums for forty years and then drops dead instantly.

98
00:10:58,080 --> 00:11:03,960
On Wishonia, we have a name for an organization that collects your money for decades and hopes you die before you can use

99
00:11:03,960 --> 00:11:11,220
it. We call it "a casino." You call it "healthcare." Their business model is dying, which is ironic given that their business

100
00:11:11,220 --> 00:11:20,240
model is about dying. Your Salvation Offer. The math they can't refuse: diabetes costs three hundred and twenty-seven billion

101
00:11:20,240 --> 00:11:27,040
dollars annually, Alzheimer's costs three hundred and fifty-five billion dollars, heart disease costs three hundred and

102
00:11:27,040 --> 00:11:31,600
sixty-three billion dollars, and cancer costs two hundred and eight billion dollars.

103
00:11:32,020 --> 00:11:36,040
Cure any one of these and the ongoing savings are roughly ninety percent.

104
00:11:36,660 --> 00:11:41,780
They don't need zero dollar costs. They just need costs to stop rising seven percent annually.

105
00:11:42,080 --> 00:11:49,000
A cure does that. A cure is, from an actuarial perspective, the best thing that can happen to an insurance company.

106
00:11:49,380 --> 00:11:55,840
The second best thing is you dying quickly. The worst thing is you dying slowly and expensively, which is what your current

107
00:11:55,840 --> 00:12:04,040
medical system specializes in. The implementation: they buy VICTORY Incentive Alignment Bonds (two hundred and seventy-two

108
00:12:04,040 --> 00:12:10,440
percent returns), they lobby for the treaty (which protects their investment), cures reduce their claims (which increases

109
00:12:10,440 --> 00:12:17,300
their profits), they lower premiums (which gives them competitive advantage), and a healthier population produces a permanent

110
00:12:17,300 --> 00:12:21,250
profit increase. Each step makes the next step more profitable.

111
00:12:21,560 --> 00:12:25,320
It's a virtuous cycle, which is like a vicious cycle but nobody dies.

112
00:12:25,860 --> 00:12:32,840
Your species has built many vicious cycles. This would be your first virtuous one, which is a statistic that should embarrass

113
00:12:32,840 --> 00:12:37,220
you more than it apparently does. Why They're Your Natural Allies.

114
00:12:38,120 --> 00:12:41,660
Insurance companies are the only industry that profits from human health.

115
00:12:42,220 --> 00:12:49,380
Every other healthcare player profits from sickness. Pharma wants chronic patients who take pills forever; insurance wants

116
00:12:49,380 --> 00:12:54,440
cures. Hospitals want full beds; insurance wants empty ones.

117
00:12:54,500 --> 00:12:58,320
Doctors want repeat visits; insurance wants one-time fixes.

118
00:12:59,160 --> 00:13:05,220
This makes insurance companies the only participant in your healthcare system whose financial incentives align with you

119
00:13:05,220 --> 00:13:12,720
not dying, which is a remarkable thing to say about a single industry in a system that supposedly exists to keep you alive.

120
00:13:14,080 --> 00:13:17,870
Pharmaceutical Companies: Converting Drug Dealers to Drug Curers.

121
00:13:18,260 --> 00:13:25,380
Pharma companies aren't evil. They're just doing what any rational organism does when you pay it to keep people sick: keeping

122
00:13:25,420 --> 00:13:33,060
people sick. They're trapped in a business model that rewards treating symptoms forever instead of curing diseases once.

123
00:13:33,860 --> 00:13:38,340
This is like blaming a river for flowing downhill. The river isn't evil.

124
00:13:38,400 --> 00:13:44,540
The river is just going where gravity takes it. If you want it to go somewhere else, you don't lecture the river.

125
00:13:44,880 --> 00:13:53,620
You move the landscape. Their Current Trap. Each new drug costs two point six billion dollars to develop.

126
00:13:54,240 --> 00:14:01,760
The success rate is ten percent, which is lottery odds, except lottery tickets don't cost billions and don't take seventeen

127
00:14:01,760 --> 00:14:08,020
years to scratch off. Revenue per success averages six point seven billion dollars.

128
00:14:08,940 --> 00:14:18,260
R.O.I. measured as internal rate of return, or I.R.R., on late-stage pipeline assets: one point two percent, which was Deloitte's

129
00:14:18,380 --> 00:14:25,720
twenty twenty-two historic low for top-twenty pharma (it bounced to five point nine percent by twenty twenty-four, still

130
00:14:25,720 --> 00:14:31,320
the kind of number that makes venture capitalists cry and then invest in real estate instead).

131
00:14:32,500 --> 00:14:40,520
Patent cliffs turn blockbusters into generics overnight. And the public hates them; Martin Shkreli is their poster boy,

132
00:14:40,660 --> 00:14:46,900
which is like having your industry represented by the person the industry itself wishes it could disown but can't because

133
00:14:46,900 --> 00:14:52,940
he's just a more honest version of what they all do. The Math That Converts Them.

134
00:14:53,480 --> 00:14:57,920
Pragmatic clinical trials become forty-four point one times cheaper.

135
00:14:58,780 --> 00:15:06,920
Current system: forty-one thousand dollars per participant on dedicated sites, with monitoring, custom infrastructure, and

136
00:15:06,920 --> 00:15:10,860
regulatory overhead that costs more than the research it's overseeing.

137
00:15:11,580 --> 00:15:19,300
With your decentralized institutes of health: cost drops roughly ninety-seven point seven percent because it uses existing

138
00:15:19,300 --> 00:15:26,100
medical practices, existing electronic health record, or E.H.R., data, and pragmatic design.

139
00:15:26,780 --> 00:15:33,960
A fifty thousand-participant trial drops from approximately two billion dollars to approximately forty-six million dollars.

140
00:15:34,340 --> 00:15:39,260
This is the difference between betting your company and betting your department's coffee budget.

141
00:15:40,380 --> 00:15:45,520
The current system costs forty-one thousand dollars per patient for trials.

142
00:15:46,120 --> 00:15:53,720
Ninety percent of drugs fail which means billions lost. Development takes ten to seventeen years, and only blockbusters

143
00:15:53,720 --> 00:16:01,800
are viable. The decentralized system costs nine hundred and twenty-nine dollars per patient (a ninety-seven point seven

144
00:16:01,800 --> 00:16:07,360
percent reduction). Failure costs tens of millions instead of billions (survivable).

145
00:16:08,560 --> 00:16:14,380
Development takes two to three years, and rare diseases become profitable because you can afford to try.

146
00:16:15,140 --> 00:16:19,620
This doesn't change whether drugs work biologically. It changes the economics.

147
00:16:19,840 --> 00:16:26,780
You profit from trying, not just from succeeding. On your planet, "profit from trying" sounds radical.

148
00:16:27,340 --> 00:16:35,060
On mine, it sounds like the definition of research. Pharma's New Business Model: Volume Over Blockbusters.

149
00:16:35,600 --> 00:16:38,600
There is lower revenue per drug, but massively more attempts.

150
00:16:39,660 --> 00:16:43,160
Currently they target one billion dollar plus blockbusters only.

151
00:16:43,520 --> 00:16:48,580
They make approximately ten attempts per year (each costing two point six billion dollars).

152
00:16:48,980 --> 00:16:52,940
They ignore rare diseases entirely, and have limited capacity.

153
00:16:53,440 --> 00:16:58,550
Under the decentralized model, five hundred million dollars per success becomes profitable.

154
00:16:58,920 --> 00:17:02,560
They can make thousands of attempts (because failure doesn't bankrupt you).

155
00:17:03,080 --> 00:17:10,260
Rare diseases become gold mines with no competition, and capacity increases by twelve point three times.

156
00:17:10,740 --> 00:17:15,060
How to Get Them Onboard. You start with generic manufacturers, who have no R.

157
00:17:15,060 --> 00:17:21,940
and D. costs and immediate margin. Then you add biotechs, who are desperate for cash and will try anything (desperation

158
00:17:21,940 --> 00:17:26,220
is the mother of innovation on your planet; this is not how it should work, but it's how it does work).

159
00:17:26,839 --> 00:17:30,540
Then big pharma follows, because they can't let competitors have the advantage.

160
00:17:31,260 --> 00:17:35,240
Then the industry transforms and competition shifts from marketing to outcomes.

161
00:17:36,240 --> 00:17:44,040
This is the same adoption curve as every technology your species has ever adopted: small, desperate, competitive, universal.

162
00:17:44,680 --> 00:17:49,000
It took seven years for smartphones. It took twelve years for social media.

163
00:17:49,560 --> 00:17:56,540
It should take less for something that cures disease, but your species has consistently demonstrated that it adopts entertainment

164
00:17:56,540 --> 00:18:03,280
faster than medicine, so add a few years for that. Politicians: Hacking Democracy's Source Code.

165
00:18:04,240 --> 00:18:11,260
Politicians want exactly two things: votes and money. I've watched your politicians for eighty years and I have never observed

166
00:18:11,260 --> 00:18:18,780
a third motivation. Some of them claim to want "a better world" or "to serve the public," but when you observe their actual

167
00:18:18,780 --> 00:18:25,700
behavior rather than their stated behavior, the data reduces to votes and money with a correlation coefficient so close

168
00:18:25,700 --> 00:18:29,000
to one point zero that the decimal places are embarrassing.

169
00:18:30,080 --> 00:18:37,280
Their Current Misery. They spend seventy percent of their time begging for money, which leaves thirty percent for everything

170
00:18:37,280 --> 00:18:43,920
else, which is governing, but most of that thirty percent is spent on things that aren't governing either, so the actual

171
00:18:43,920 --> 00:18:48,020
time spent governing approaches zero, which explains the quality of the governance.

172
00:18:48,880 --> 00:18:55,340
Their approval rating is eighteen percent, which means eighty-two percent of the people they work for think they're doing

173
00:18:55,340 --> 00:19:03,080
a bad job, which would get you fired in any other profession, but in politics it gets you re-elected because the other candidate

174
00:19:03,080 --> 00:19:09,300
has an eighteen percent approval rating too, and the election is just a competition to see who is less disliked.

175
00:19:10,840 --> 00:19:18,600
Lobbyists write the bills. Donors set the agenda. The politician's actual role is to stand in front of a flag and announce

176
00:19:18,600 --> 00:19:24,500
things that other people decided, which is called "leadership" on your planet and "puppetry" on mine.

177
00:19:25,480 --> 00:19:32,440
Your Political Welfare Program. The money pipeline: support the treaty and Super PAC funds flow in.

178
00:19:33,040 --> 00:19:40,140
Oppose it and Super PAC funds flow to your opponent. This is how your political system already works, except currently the

179
00:19:40,140 --> 00:19:44,100
money flows from death manufacturers who want more weapons.

180
00:19:44,240 --> 00:19:48,360
You're just adding a second faucet that flows from people who want fewer diseases.

181
00:19:49,040 --> 00:19:54,350
The politician doesn't even need to change their behavior. They just need to notice which faucet has more water.

182
00:19:54,900 --> 00:20:00,420
The vote harvest: two hundred and eighty million people voting on Wishocracy.

183
00:20:01,020 --> 00:20:04,600
You publish voting records ("Senator X opposes cheaper healthcare").

184
00:20:05,040 --> 00:20:10,490
Their constituents see this in real-time. Support the treaty or face electoral extinction.

185
00:20:11,500 --> 00:20:19,620
This is democracy working as designed, which is so unusual that it will probably be reported as a threat to democracy, because

186
00:20:19,620 --> 00:20:26,740
on your planet, democracy working is considered a disruption to the normal functioning of democracy, which is not working.

187
00:20:27,940 --> 00:20:34,240
The Bipartisan Beauty. Republicans will call it a free market solution that cuts government spending.

188
00:20:34,980 --> 00:20:38,720
Democrats will call it universal healthcare access that helps the poor.

189
00:20:39,400 --> 00:20:43,520
Libertarians will call it the end of F.D.A. tyranny and personal freedom.

190
00:20:44,380 --> 00:20:48,840
Socialists will call it the destruction of Big Pharma monopoly and power to the people.

191
00:20:49,640 --> 00:20:53,900
They're all describing the same thing. They're all right. And they'll all take credit.

192
00:20:54,720 --> 00:21:01,780
This is the only policy in your species' history that every political faction can honestly claim as their own, which means

193
00:21:01,780 --> 00:21:08,560
it's either the best policy ever proposed or the most dishonest, and since your species can't tell the difference, it doesn't

194
00:21:08,560 --> 00:21:13,780
matter. Billionaires: Self-Interest Meets Self-Preservation.

195
00:21:14,820 --> 00:21:20,340
Billionaires have everything except the one thing money can't buy: not dying.

196
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:25,340
A billion dollars and a body that rots at the same rate as everyone else's.

197
00:21:26,180 --> 00:21:29,920
On Wishonia, we find this the most fascinating thing about your wealthy.

198
00:21:30,580 --> 00:21:39,040
They accumulate resources for decades, building empires, optimizing returns, diversifying portfolios, and then their cells

199
00:21:39,040 --> 00:21:46,280
malfunction and they die like everyone else. It's as if a dragon spent its whole life hoarding gold and then choked on a

200
00:21:46,280 --> 00:21:55,860
coin. The irony would be poetic if it weren't so common. The package: VICTORY Incentive Alignment Bonds funded by treaty

201
00:21:55,860 --> 00:22:04,700
revenue, beating traditional portfolio yields (returns). "The Gates Cancer Center" and a Nobel Peace Prize and history books

202
00:22:04,700 --> 00:22:12,420
reading "Funded the cure for death" (legacy). And the part they won't say out loud but think every time their knee hurts:

203
00:22:12,940 --> 00:22:20,080
they're getting old, their genetics aren't special, and every disease cured is their lifespan extended (survival).

204
00:22:21,380 --> 00:22:25,260
Your species' billionaires have been funding vanity projects for decades.

205
00:22:25,980 --> 00:22:31,740
Space tourism. Underwater hotels. Islands. None of these extend their lifespan.

206
00:22:32,320 --> 00:22:36,890
This does. It's the first vanity project where the vanity is rational.

207
00:22:37,680 --> 00:22:46,440
The Domino Effect. Insurance companies move first, because pure financial logic is their entire personality and curing disease

208
00:22:46,440 --> 00:22:54,460
reduces their costs. Their lobbying convinces politicians, because money talks and insurance money talks loudly.

209
00:22:55,240 --> 00:23:03,940
Politicians create treaty momentum, because votes follow money follows lobbying follows insurance follows actuarial tables,

210
00:23:04,360 --> 00:23:09,880
which is the most boring causal chain in history but also the most reliable.

211
00:23:11,000 --> 00:23:16,340
Defense contractors hedge, because they can't risk being left out of a profitable shift.

212
00:23:17,100 --> 00:23:24,190
Pharma pivots, because adapt or die, which is ironic advice to give an industry that's supposed to prevent dying.

213
00:23:25,160 --> 00:23:33,560
Billionaires pile in last, because FOMO is powerful in humans generally and devastating in billionaires specifically, who

214
00:23:33,560 --> 00:23:38,740
have built their identities around being first and cannot tolerate being last.

215
00:23:40,020 --> 00:23:43,980
Why This Can Work: The Coordination Problem Has a Price Tag.

216
00:23:45,300 --> 00:23:49,800
Public choice theory predicts that concentrated interests beat diffuse interests.

217
00:23:50,780 --> 00:23:59,820
Always. Ten lobbyists who each make ten million dollars from war will outfight ten million patients who each lose ten dollars

218
00:23:59,820 --> 00:24:07,840
from disease. This is Olson's observation, and it's correct, and it's the reason your species builds weapons better than

219
00:24:07,840 --> 00:24:15,560
it cures cancer. The part Olson didn't account for: the people getting screwed have ninety times more money.

220
00:24:16,320 --> 00:24:24,580
Concentrated interests (defense, fossil fuels) have roughly five trillion dollars in market cap and spend one hundred million

221
00:24:24,580 --> 00:24:33,660
to one billion dollars annually on political influence. Diffuse beneficiaries (everyone who'd benefit from cures) have four

222
00:24:33,660 --> 00:24:41,380
hundred and fifty-four trillion dollars in household wealth and spend approximately zero dollars on political influence,

223
00:24:41,880 --> 00:24:48,000
because of coordination failure, which is the academic term for "everyone wants it but nobody does anything.".

224
00:24:49,100 --> 00:24:54,560
Having more capital doesn't help if you can't deploy it. That's Olson's whole point and he's right.

225
00:24:55,160 --> 00:25:02,040
A ninety to one capital advantage means nothing when each individual's share is too small to motivate action.

226
00:25:02,940 --> 00:25:10,840
Coordination costs eat everything. This is why your species can't have nice things, stated in the language of economics

227
00:25:10,840 --> 00:25:18,920
so that economists will take it seriously. Incentive Alignment Bonds are a coordination mechanism disguised as a financial

228
00:25:18,920 --> 00:25:26,510
product. They turn political change into an investment. Investors don't need to care about curing disease.

229
00:25:26,920 --> 00:25:32,580
They need to care about returns. The returns happen to require curing disease.

230
00:25:33,120 --> 00:25:40,980
Whether this actually works at scale is an open question (nobody's tried it), but the logic is: if you can make "lobby for

231
00:25:40,980 --> 00:25:46,180
health" pay better than "lobby for war," lobbyists will lobby for health.

232
00:25:47,020 --> 00:25:54,220
This is not a controversial claim about human nature. This is the least controversial claim about human nature.

233
00:25:54,260 --> 00:25:59,040
It is, in fact, the only claim about human nature that has never been wrong.

234
00:26:00,040 --> 00:26:08,600
Why the cascade can happen (not "must" happen). Once I.A.B.s concentrate financial benefits on the pro-health side, politicians

235
00:26:08,600 --> 00:26:14,240
face a new calculation where I.A.B. benefits outweigh concentrated opposition costs.

236
00:26:15,160 --> 00:26:20,540
Each policy success increases I.A.B. funding, which strengthens the coalition.

237
00:26:20,740 --> 00:26:24,320
Investors lobby for treaty expansion to increase their returns.

238
00:26:24,500 --> 00:26:30,120
The dynamic reverses: supporting clinical trial funding becomes the concentrated-benefit option.

239
00:26:30,800 --> 00:26:35,480
The diffuse interest becomes concentrated. The Olsonian problem dissolves.

240
00:26:35,540 --> 00:26:40,540
Not because humans got better. Because the math changed. This isn't inevitable.

241
00:26:40,700 --> 00:26:48,460
It requires sufficient I.A.B. funding to overcome opposition, and "sufficient" is a number nobody can calculate in advance.

242
00:26:49,060 --> 00:26:55,820
But the capital exists. It just needs a reason to move. On Wishonia, we'd call this "providing a reason."

243
00:26:56,420 --> 00:27:03,020
On Earth, you'd call it "the most important financial product in history" or "a pipe dream," depending on whether you've

244
00:27:03,020 --> 00:27:11,660
read the math. Why This Scales: The Ratchet Effect. Each treaty expansion creates more political incentive funding to lobby

245
00:27:11,660 --> 00:27:18,580
for the next expansion. At one percent, political incentive funding is two point seven two billion dollars per year.

246
00:27:19,100 --> 00:27:24,820
At ten percent, that scales to ten times. Politicians who supported the one percent treaty get rewarded.

247
00:27:25,380 --> 00:27:32,840
Those rewards grow if they push for two percent. It's a ratchet that only moves in one direction: the direction where people

248
00:27:32,840 --> 00:27:37,520
stop dying. You've built ratchets like this before. Social Security.

249
00:27:37,580 --> 00:27:44,360
Medicare. Once they exist, dismantling them is politically suicidal because too many people depend on them.

250
00:27:44,940 --> 00:27:51,600
The difference is you built those by accident, through crisis and desperation, and spent decades arguing about whether they

251
00:27:51,600 --> 00:27:59,260
were good ideas. This time, try doing it on purpose. On Wishonia, we've found that building things on purpose works better

252
00:27:59,260 --> 00:28:05,860
than building them by accident, though I realize this is a controversial position for a species that invented penicillin

253
00:28:05,860 --> 00:28:06,940
by leaving a sandwich out.
